IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cpp-core`
IMPORT `cp6-schedules`

-- Tests of cp6-schedules.l4 (Schedules 1 and 2 of the Act, lines 3101 to 3136).
-- Every expected value is typed from the printed Schedule (the percent columns), not from the module.

§ `Tests -- Schedules 1 and 2`

§§ `Schedule 1: each printed row, columns employees, employers, self-employed persons`

#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1987 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 1.9%, 1.9%, 3.8%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1988 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.0%, 2.0%, 4.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1989 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.1%, 2.1%, 4.2%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1990 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.2%, 2.2%, 4.4%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1991 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.3%, 2.3%, 4.6%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1992 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.4%, 2.4%, 4.8%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1993 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.5%, 2.5%, 5.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1994 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.6%, 2.6%, 5.2%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1995 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.7%, 2.7%, 5.4%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1996 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 2.8%, 2.8%, 5.6%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1997 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 3.0%, 3.0%, 6.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1998 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 3.2%, 3.2%, 6.4%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1999 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 3.5%, 3.5%, 7.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 2000 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 3.9%, 3.9%, 7.8%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 2001 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 4.3%, 4.3%, 8.6%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 2002 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 4.7%, 4.7%, 9.4%)

§§ `Schedule 1: the last printed row, "2003 and each subsequent year", and the year before the table`

#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 2003 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 4.95%, 4.95%, 9.9%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 2004 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 4.95%, 4.95%, 9.9%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 2030 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 4.95%, 4.95%, 9.9%)
-- Not covered: the table starts at 1987, so 1986 is not answered (an explicit NOTHING, not a rate of 0).
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` 1986 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the base contribution rate of a capacity for a year` `contributing as an employee` 1986 EQUALS NOTHING

§§ `Schedule 1: the rate of one capacity`

#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the base contribution rate of a capacity for a year` `contributing as an employee` 2002 EQUALS JUST (4.7%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the base contribution rate of a capacity for a year` `contributing as an employer` 2002 EQUALS JUST (4.7%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the base contribution rate of a capacity for a year` `contributing as a self-employed person` 2002 EQUALS JUST (9.4%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the base contribution rate of a capacity for a year` `contributing as an employee` 2003 EQUALS JUST (4.95%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the base contribution rate of a capacity for a year` `contributing as a self-employed person` 2003 EQUALS JUST (9.9%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 — the base contribution rate of a capacity for a year` `contributing as a self-employed person` 2026 EQUALS JUST (9.9%)

§§ `Schedule 2: the first additional rates`

#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2018 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2019 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 0.15%, 0.15%, 0.3%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2020 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 0.3%, 0.3%, 0.6%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2021 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 0.5%, 0.5%, 1.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2022 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 0.75%, 0.75%, 1.5%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2023 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 1.0%, 1.0%, 2.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2024 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 1.0%, 1.0%, 2.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` 2040 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 1.0%, 1.0%, 2.0%)

§§ `Schedule 2: the second additional rates`

#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of second additional rates for a year` 2023 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of second additional rates for a year` 2024 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 4.0%, 4.0%, 8.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 2 — the row of second additional rates for a year` 2050 EQUALS JUST (`cp6 Row of a schedule of contribution rates` OF 4.0%, 4.0%, 8.0%)

§§ `The rate of a tier and a capacity`

#ASSERT `Schedule 1 and 2 — the contribution rate of a tier and a capacity for a year` `the base tier` `contributing as an employee` 2003 EQUALS JUST (4.95%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 and 2 — the contribution rate of a tier and a capacity for a year` `the first additional tier` `contributing as an employer` 2019 EQUALS JUST (0.15%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 and 2 — the contribution rate of a tier and a capacity for a year` `the first additional tier` `contributing as a self-employed person` 2022 EQUALS JUST (1.5%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 and 2 — the contribution rate of a tier and a capacity for a year` `the first additional tier` `contributing as an employee` 2018 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 and 2 — the contribution rate of a tier and a capacity for a year` `the second additional tier` `contributing as an employee` 2023 EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 and 2 — the contribution rate of a tier and a capacity for a year` `the second additional tier` `contributing as an employer` 2024 EQUALS JUST (4.0%)
#ASSERT `Schedule 1 and 2 — the contribution rate of a tier and a capacity for a year` `the second additional tier` `contributing as a self-employed person` 2024 EQUALS JUST (8.0%)

§§ `A finding: in every printed row the self-employed rate is the employee rate plus the employer rate`

-- Section 113.1(7)(a) and (b) require this of any change to the rates; the printed rows already satisfy it.
-- The test reads the rows through the rules, with the sums worked out here from the printed rows.
GIVEN years IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`every Schedule 1 row has the self-employed rate equal to the employee rate plus the employer rate` years MEANS
    all (GIVEN y YIELD
            CONSIDER `Schedule 1 — the row of rates for a year` y
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST row THEN row's `schedule rate for self-employed persons` EQUALS (row's `schedule rate for employees`) PLUS (row's `schedule rate for employers`)) years

#ASSERT `every Schedule 1 row has the self-employed rate equal to the employee rate plus the employer rate` (LIST 1987, 1988, 1989, 1990, 1991, 1992, 1993, 1994, 1995, 1996, 1997, 1998, 1999, 2000, 2001, 2002, 2003, 2010, 2030)

GIVEN years IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`every first additional row has the self-employed rate equal to the employee rate plus the employer rate` years MEANS
    all (GIVEN y YIELD
            CONSIDER `Schedule 2 — the row of first additional rates for a year` y
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST row THEN row's `schedule rate for self-employed persons` EQUALS (row's `schedule rate for employees`) PLUS (row's `schedule rate for employers`)) years

#ASSERT `every first additional row has the self-employed rate equal to the employee rate plus the employer rate` (LIST 2019, 2020, 2021, 2022, 2023, 2024, 2030)

GIVEN years IS A LIST OF NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`every second additional row has the self-employed rate equal to the employee rate plus the employer rate` years MEANS
    all (GIVEN y YIELD
            CONSIDER `Schedule 2 — the row of second additional rates for a year` y
            WHEN NOTHING THEN FALSE
            WHEN JUST row THEN row's `schedule rate for self-employed persons` EQUALS (row's `schedule rate for employees`) PLUS (row's `schedule rate for employers`)) years

#ASSERT `every second additional row has the self-employed rate equal to the employee rate plus the employer rate` (LIST 2024, 2030)
